# 4. Architectural Goals

The architectural goals are to:

- establish coherent documentary contracts and terminology;
- separate primary responsibilities while permitting explicit collaboration;
- preserve stable logical identity for applicable normalized documents;
- enable discovery under actual catalog, relationship and source evidence;
- support subject-specific governance and exact-edition traceability;
- preserve explicit immutable snapshot selections and history;
- support assessed incremental evolution and justified necessary change;
- avoid unnecessary implementation-technology prescriptions.

Achieved quality requires identified artifacts/editions, applicable requirements, checks, observations, executor, coverage and limits. Goals alone prove neither approval, publication, membership nor implemented capability.

---

# 3. Scope

This document defines the architecture of the Classification component.

It specifies:

- the architectural position of Classification;
- the purpose of Classification;
- the responsibilities of the component;
- its architectural boundaries;
- its dependencies;
- its relationships with other components;
- the architectural principles governing its evolution.

It does not define:

- individual classification concepts;
- the classification model;
- classification elements;
- classification schema;
- classification lifecycle;
- validation rules;
- governance rules;
- relationships between classification elements;
- classification constraints;
- extensibility mechanisms;
- best practices;
- classification dictionary;
- complete classification specification;
- classification examples.

Those subjects are defined by the subsequent documents of the Classification component.

---

# 34. Schema Cardinality

The conceptual schema identifies the three Classification elements.

However, the currently established source material does not define authoritative cardinality rules specifying whether:

- every element must always have a value;
- some elements may be omitted;
- multiple values may occur;
- a Classification may contain additional elements.

Therefore, this document does not introduce such rules as normative requirements.

Those rules must be established through the Classification Constraints and Validation documents if required.

---

# 5. Validation Responsibility

Classification Validation is responsible for:

- evaluating Classification;
- detecting violations;
- reporting conformance;
- identifying inconsistencies;
- supporting Classification quality.

It is not responsible for:

- correcting Classification;
- generating Classification;
- defining Classification vocabulary;
- approving Classification;
- governing Classification evolution.

The separation follows the established Metadata architecture, where Validation evaluates conformance while Governance makes acceptance decisions. 
---

# 42. Invalid

A Classification is **Invalid** when one or more applicable rules produce a blocking violation.

Examples may include:

- undefined Family;
- undefined Type;
- invalid Family-Type combination;
- prohibited structural element;
- incompatible Classification values.

---

# 5. Governance Objectives

Classification Governance has the following objectives:

- preserve Classification integrity;
- preserve semantic consistency;
- control Classification evolution;
- authorize Classification changes;
- maintain architectural compliance;
- preserve compatibility whenever reasonably possible;
- maintain a canonical Classification vocabulary;
- control Classification deprecation and retirement;
- preserve historical Classification meaning;
- support long-term interoperability.

---

# 7. Governance Responsibilities

Classification Governance is responsible for:

- evaluating Classification changes;
- approving Classification evolution;
- preserving Classification architectural integrity;
- maintaining semantic consistency;
- authorizing Classification lifecycle transitions;
- managing Classification deprecation;
- managing Classification retirement;
- authorizing publication of canonical Classification definitions;
- maintaining Classification consistency;
- controlling Classification extensions.

---

# 62. Relationship Migration

When relationship semantics change, migration may be required.

Migration should identify:

- affected relationships;
- affected Classification Elements;
- affected documents;
- affected consumers;
- historical impact;
- compatibility requirements.

Migration is governed separately from the semantic definition of the relationship.

---

# 12. Family Constraints

A Family represents a high-level Classification domain.

A Family shall:

- have a unique canonical identity;
- have a defined semantic meaning;
- not duplicate another Family semantically;
- remain independent of implementation;
- participate only in permitted relationships.

---

# 13. Type Constraints

A Type specializes Classification within a Family context.

A Type shall:

- have a unique canonical identity;
- have a defined semantic meaning;
- belong to a permitted Family;
- not duplicate another Type within the same semantic context;
- comply with the Classification Schema.

---

# 14. Class Constraints

A Class provides a more specific Classification level within a Type context.

A Class shall:

- have a unique canonical identity;
- have a defined semantic meaning;
- belong to a permitted Type;
- comply with applicable constraints;
- not introduce semantic duplication.

---

# 26. Classification Element Identity Constraints

Every canonical Classification Element shall possess a stable identity.

The identity shall be:

- unique within the applicable scope;
- stable;
- traceable;
- independent of physical representation.

Changing the physical representation of a Classification Element shall not implicitly create a new conceptual element.

---

# 27. Identity Stability

Classification Element identity shall remain stable throughout its lifecycle unless Governance explicitly authorizes a replacement.

The following are not, by themselves, identity changes:

- renaming a file;
- changing storage;
- changing serialization;
- changing repository;
- changing implementation.

---

# 28. Cardinality Constraints

Classification cardinality defines the permitted number of relationships or elements within a structural context.

Cardinality may constrain:

- number of parents;
- number of children;
- number of relationships;
- number of applicable Classification Elements.

Cardinality shall be defined independently of implementation.

---

# 2. Purpose

The purpose of Classification Best Practices is to provide practical guidance for maintaining a high-quality Classification architecture.

The practices defined here aim to:

- preserve semantic clarity;
- reduce unnecessary complexity;
- prevent semantic duplication;
- maintain structural coherence;
- improve discoverability;
- support governance;
- facilitate validation;
- support controlled evolution;
- preserve long-term maintainability.

---

# 1. Introduction

The Classification Dictionary defines the canonical terminology used by the Classification component of the Documentation Framework.

Classification provides the semantic organization through which documents are grouped into meaningful categories.

The Dictionary establishes a common vocabulary for the concepts, elements, relationships and supporting terms used by Classification.

Its purpose is to ensure that the same terminology is used consistently across:

- Classification documents;
- document metadata;
- validation;
- governance;
- relationships;
- extensions;
- specifications;
- examples;
- automation.

The Classification Dictionary is therefore a terminology reference.

It does not replace the Classification Model, Schema, Constraints or Specification.

---

# 3. Scope

The Dictionary covers terminology associated with:

- Classification;
- Classification structure;
- Classification elements;
- Classification relationships;
- Classification schema;
- Classification lifecycle;
- Classification validation;
- Classification governance;
- Classification constraints;
- Classification extensibility.

It also defines the terminology necessary to describe the use and maintenance of Classification.

The Dictionary does not define the complete normative behavior of Classification.

That responsibility belongs to the applicable architectural and specification documents.

---

## 6.18. R-018 — Responsibility Separation

Classification shall not assume responsibilities belonging to other Documentation Framework components.

In particular, Classification shall not use its hierarchy as a substitute for:

- Document Identity;
- Metadata;
- Lifecycle;
- Governance;
- Registry;
- Relationships;
- Knowledge Graph.

---

# 2. Purpose

The purpose of this document is to provide concrete examples of Classification usage.

The examples support:

- understanding the Classification model;
- understanding Classification terminology;
- illustrating Classification structures;
- demonstrating Classification assignments;
- demonstrating relationships;
- demonstrating validation;
- demonstrating lifecycle transitions;
- demonstrating governance;
- demonstrating extensibility;
- illustrating correct and incorrect Classification practices.

---

# 5. Architectural Purpose

The Metadata component provides standardized descriptive information that characterizes documents independently of their content.

Metadata enables every document to become:

- self-describing;
- uniquely identifiable;
- semantically classifiable;
- governable;
- discoverable;
- traceable;
- automatable.

Metadata does not replace document content.

Instead, it complements document content by providing architectural information about the document itself.

---

# 6. Responsibilities

The Metadata component is responsible for:

- describing documents through standardized Metadata;
- providing descriptive information for architectural components;
- supporting document identification;
- supporting document classification;
- supporting governance processes;
- supporting discovery mechanisms;
- supporting architectural traceability;
- supporting automation activities.

Metadata shall not perform responsibilities assigned to other architectural components.

---

# 8. Architectural Dependencies

## 8.1. Depends On

The Metadata component depends only upon the Foundation and Architecture layers.

It shall not depend upon Governance, Discovery, Automation or Integration.

---

## 8.2. Required By

Metadata provides architectural support for:

- Document Identity;
- Classification;
- Workflow;
- Policies;
- Baselines;
- Registry;
- Relationships;
- Knowledge Graph;
- Validation;
- Automation;
- AI Integration.

These components consume Metadata but do not own it.

---

# 14. Conformance

An implementation conforms to the Metadata Architecture when:

- Metadata is maintained independently of document content;
- Metadata responsibilities remain isolated from other components;
- Metadata follows the Documentation Framework terminology;
- Metadata respects the Documentation Layer boundaries;
- Metadata remains technology independent;
- Metadata is extensible without architectural disruption.

---

# 5. Architectural Purpose

The Templates component provides reusable structural definitions for documentation.

A template establishes a standardized organization that may define, where applicable:

* document sections;
* structural elements;
* metadata locations;
* classification locations;
* references;
* content placeholders;
* instructions;
* structural rules;
* structural constraints.

Templates allow recurring documentation structures to be defined once and reused consistently.

The component therefore contributes to:

* structural consistency;
* documentation reuse;
* predictable organization;
* maintainability;
* validation;
* documentation generation;
* controlled structural evolution.

---

# 6. Responsibilities

The Templates component is responsible for:

* defining template architecture;
* defining template concepts;
* defining the template model;
* defining template elements;
* defining template structures;
* defining template schemas;
* defining template lifecycle;
* defining template validation;
* defining template governance;
* defining template relationships;
* defining template constraints;
* defining template extensibility;
* defining reusable documentation structures;
* defining authoritative template terminology;
* supporting standardized documentation creation;
* supporting structural conformance.

Templates shall provide structural definitions without redefining concepts owned by other architectural components.

---

# 7. Non-Responsibilities

The Templates component shall not:

* assign permanent document identities;
* define document identity semantics;
* define Metadata semantics;
* define Metadata ownership;
* classify documents;
* define classification semantics;
* manage document repositories;
* manage document storage;
* manage document permissions;
* manage document workflow;
* govern organizational policies;
* maintain document registries;
* define knowledge graph semantics;
* implement document generation;
* prescribe implementation technologies.

Templates may provide structures for representing information owned by other components, but ownership of that information remains with the corresponding component.

---

# 8. Architectural Boundaries

The Templates component is bounded by the distinction between **document structure** and the semantic or operational responsibilities of other components.

Templates define how documentation may be structurally organized.

They do not determine:

* what permanently identifies a document;
* what metadata means;
* how documents are classified;
* where document instances are stored;
* how documents progress through workflows;
* how documents are governed operationally;
* how documents are discovered or indexed.

This separation preserves component independence and prevents structural definitions from becoming substitutes for other architectural responsibilities.

---

# 2. Purpose

The purpose of this roadmap is to define:

- the implementation sequence of the Classification component;
- the progressive introduction of classification capabilities;
- the evolution of classification structures;
- dependencies and integration with other architectural components;
- long-term classification governance.

---

# 3. Scope

This roadmap covers:

- classification architecture;
- classification concepts;
- classification models;
- classification schemes;
- classification rules;
- classification validation;
- classification governance;
- classification extensibility.

It does not define:

- Document Identity;
- Metadata;
- Templates;
- document content;
- document storage;
- document workflows.

These responsibilities belong to other Documentation Framework components.

---

# 4. Responsibility

The Classification component is responsible for:

- defining classification concepts;
- defining classification models;
- defining classification elements;
- defining classification schemas;
- defining classification assignments;
- defining classification relationships;
- defining classification constraints;
- defining classification lifecycle;
- defining classification validation;
- defining classification governance;
- defining classification extensibility;
- defining authoritative classification terminology.

Classification does not define:

- document identity;
- document metadata in general;
- document templates;
- document workflows;
- physical repository organization.

Those responsibilities belong to other components of the Documentation Framework.

---

# 11. Validation

Classification shall support validation at multiple levels:

1. element existence;
2. structural validity;
3. value validity;
4. semantic validity;
5. cross-element consistency;
6. cross-document consistency;
7. architectural consistency.

The detailed validation rules are defined by **DF-CLS-007 — Classification Validation**.

---

# 12. Governance

Classification systems shall be subject to controlled governance.

Governance concerns include:

- ownership;
- responsibility;
- approval;
- change control;
- review;
- publication;
- deprecation;
- retirement.

The detailed governance model is defined by **DF-CLS-008 — Classification Governance**.

---

# 18. Component Completion

The Classification component is considered architecturally complete when its documentation establishes:

- the architectural responsibility of Classification;
- its fundamental concepts;
- its conceptual model;
- its elements;
- its schema;
- its lifecycle;
- its validation rules;
- its governance;
- its relationships;
- its constraints;
- its extensibility mechanisms;
- its best practices;
- its authoritative terminology;
- its normative specification;
- representative examples.

---
